// Ddgmm This function performs the matrix-matrix multiplication C={A×diag(X)if mode == CUBLAS_SIDE_RIGHTdiag(X)×Aif mode == [SideLeft] where A and C are matrices stored in column-major format with dimensions m×n .
//
// X is a vector of size n if mode == [SideRight] and of size m if mode == [SideLeft]. X is gathered from one-dimensional array x with stride incx. The absolute value of incx is the stride and the sign of incx is direction of the stride. If incx is positive, then we forward x from the first element. Otherwise, we backward x from the last element. The formula of X is Xj={x [j×incx]if incx≥0x[(χ−1)×|incx|−j×|incx|]if incx<0 where χ=m if mode == [SideLeft] and χ=n if mode == [SideRight]. Example 1: if the user wants to perform diag(diag(B))×A , then incx=ldb+1 where ldb is leading dimension of matrix B, either row-major or column-major. Example 2: if the user wants to perform α×A , then there are two choices, either cublasgeam() with *beta == 0 and transa == [OpN] or cublasdgmm() with incx == 0 and x [0] == alpha. The operation is out-of-place. The in-place only works if lda == ldc. The possible error values returned by this function and their meanings are listed below.
//
//   - mode: Left multiply if mode == [SideLeft] or right multiply if mode == [SideRight]
//   - m: Number of rows of matrix A and C
//   - n: Number of columns of matrix A and C
//   - A: < type > array of dimensions lda x n with lda >= max(1, m)
//   - lda: Leading dimension of two-dimensional array used to store the matrix A
//   - x: One-dimensional < type > array of size abs(incx) x m if mode == [SideLeft] and abs(incx) x n if mode == [SideRight]
//   - incx: Stride of one-dimensional array x
//   - C: < type > array of dimensions ldc x n with ldc >= max(1, m)
//   - ldc: Leading dimension of a two-dimensional array used to store the matrix C
//
// Returns:
//   - StatusExecutionFailed: The function failed to launch on the GPU
//   - StatusInvalidValue: - If m < 0 or n < 0, or - if mode is not one of [SideLeft] and [SideRight], or - if lda < max(1, m), or - if ldc < max(1, m)
//   - StatusNotInitialized: The library was not initialized
//   - StatusSuccess: The operation completed successfully
//
// Wraps cublasDdgmm.
func (h Handle) Ddgmm(mode SideMode, m int32, n int32, A *float64, lda int32, x *float64, incx int32, C *float64, ldc int32) error {
	return ddgmm(h, mode, m, n, A, lda, x, incx, C, ldc)
}

// Ddgmm64 This function performs the matrix-matrix multiplication C={A×diag(X)if mode == CUBLAS_SIDE_RIGHTdiag(X)×Aif mode == [SideLeft] where A and C are matrices stored in column-major format with dimensions m×n .
//
// X is a vector of size n if mode == [SideRight] and of size m if mode == [SideLeft]. X is gathered from one-dimensional array x with stride incx. The absolute value of incx is the stride and the sign of incx is direction of the stride. If incx is positive, then we forward x from the first element. Otherwise, we backward x from the last element. The formula of X is Xj={x [j×incx]if incx≥0x[(χ−1)×|incx|−j×|incx|]if incx<0 where χ=m if mode == [SideLeft] and χ=n if mode == [SideRight]. Example 1: if the user wants to perform diag(diag(B))×A , then incx=ldb+1 where ldb is leading dimension of matrix B, either row-major or column-major. Example 2: if the user wants to perform α×A , then there are two choices, either cublasgeam() with *beta == 0 and transa == [OpN] or cublasdgmm() with incx == 0 and x [0] == alpha. The operation is out-of-place. The in-place only works if lda == ldc. The possible error values returned by this function and their meanings are listed below.
//
//   - mode: Left multiply if mode == [SideLeft] or right multiply if mode == [SideRight]
//   - m: Number of rows of matrix A and C
//   - n: Number of columns of matrix A and C
//   - A: < type > array of dimensions lda x n with lda >= max(1, m)
//   - lda: Leading dimension of two-dimensional array used to store the matrix A
//   - x: One-dimensional < type > array of size abs(incx) x m if mode == [SideLeft] and abs(incx) x n if mode == [SideRight]
//   - incx: Stride of one-dimensional array x
//   - C: < type > array of dimensions ldc x n with ldc >= max(1, m)
//   - ldc: Leading dimension of a two-dimensional array used to store the matrix C
//
// Returns:
//   - StatusExecutionFailed: The function failed to launch on the GPU
//   - StatusInvalidValue: - If m < 0 or n < 0, or - if mode is not one of [SideLeft] and [SideRight], or - if lda < max(1, m), or - if ldc < max(1, m)
//   - StatusNotInitialized: The library was not initialized
//   - StatusSuccess: The operation completed successfully
//
// Wraps cublasDdgmm_64.
func (h Handle) Ddgmm64(mode SideMode, m int64, n int64, A *float64, lda int64, x *float64, incx int64, C *float64, ldc int64) error {
	return ddgmm64(h, mode, m, n, A, lda, x, incx, C, ldc)
}

// Ddot This function computes the dot product of vectors x and y.
//
// Hence, the result is ∑i=1n(xk×yj) where k=1+(i−1)∗incx and j=1+(i−1)∗incy . Notice that in the first equation the conjugate of the element of vector x should be used if the function name ends in character ‘c’ and that the last two equations reflect 1-based indexing used for compatibility with Fortran. The possible error values returned by this function and their meanings are listed below. For references please refer to NETLIB documentation: sdot(), ddot(), cdotu(), cdotc(), zdotu(), zdotc()
//
//   - n: Number of elements in the vectors x and y
//   - x: < type > vector with n elements
//   - incx: Stride between consecutive elements of x
//   - y: < type > vector with n elements
//   - incy: Stride between consecutive elements of y
//
// Returns:
//   - StatusAllocFailed: The reduction buffer could not be allocated
//   - StatusExecutionFailed: The function failed to launch on the GPU
//   - StatusNotInitialized: The library was not initialized
//   - StatusSuccess: The operation completed successfully
//
// Wraps cublasDdot_v2.
func (h Handle) Ddot(n int32, x *float64, incx int32, y *float64, incy int32) (float64, error) {
	return ddot(h, n, x, incx, y, incy)
}

// Ddot64 This function computes the dot product of vectors x and y.
//
// Hence, the result is ∑i=1n(xk×yj) where k=1+(i−1)∗incx and j=1+(i−1)∗incy . Notice that in the first equation the conjugate of the element of vector x should be used if the function name ends in character ‘c’ and that the last two equations reflect 1-based indexing used for compatibility with Fortran. The possible error values returned by this function and their meanings are listed below. For references please refer to NETLIB documentation: sdot(), ddot(), cdotu(), cdotc(), zdotu(), zdotc()
//
//   - n: Number of elements in the vectors x and y
//   - x: < type > vector with n elements
//   - incx: Stride between consecutive elements of x
//   - y: < type > vector with n elements
//   - incy: Stride between consecutive elements of y
//
// Returns:
//   - StatusAllocFailed: The reduction buffer could not be allocated
//   - StatusExecutionFailed: The function failed to launch on the GPU
//   - StatusNotInitialized: The library was not initialized
//   - StatusSuccess: The operation completed successfully
//
// Wraps cublasDdot_v2_64.
func (h Handle) Ddot64(n int64, x *float64, incx int64, y *float64, incy int64) (float64, error) {
	return ddot64(h, n, x, incx, y, incy)
}

// Destroy This function releases hardware resources used by the cuBLAS library.
//
// This function is usually the last call with a particular handle to the cuBLAS library. Because [Create] allocates some internal resources and the release of those resources by calling [Handle.Destroy] will implicitly call cudaDeviceSynchronize(), it is recommended to minimize the number of times these functions are called.
//
// Wraps cublasDestroy_v2.
func (h Handle) Destroy() error {
	return destroy(h)
}

// Dgbmv This function performs the banded matrix-vector multiplication y=α op(A)x+βy where A is a banded matrix with kl subdiagonals and ku superdiagonals, x and y are vectors, and α and β are scalars.
//
// Also, for matrix A op(A)={A if trans == CUBLAS_OP_NAT if trans == CUBLAS_OP_TAH if trans == [OpC] The banded matrix A is stored column by column, with the main diagonal stored in row ku+1 (starting in first position), the first superdiagonal stored in row ku (starting in second position), the first subdiagonal stored in row ku+2 (starting in first position), etc. So that in general, the element A(i,j) is stored in the memory location A(ku+1+i-j,j) for j=1,…,n and i∈[max(1,j−ku),min(m,j+kl)] . Also, the elements in the array A that do not conceptually correspond to the elements in the banded matrix (the top left ku×ku and bottom right kl×kl triangles) are not referenced. The possible error values returned by this function and their meanings are listed below. For references please refer to NETLIB documentation: sgbmv(), dgbmv(), cgbmv(), zgbmv()
//
//   - trans: Operation op( A) that is non- or (conj.) transpose
//   - m: Number of rows of matrix A
//   - n: Number of columns of matrix A
//   - kl: Number of subdiagonals of matrix A
//   - ku: Number of superdiagonals of matrix A
//   - alpha: < type > scalar used for multiplication
//   - A: < type > array of dimension lda x n with lda >= kl + ku + 1
//   - lda: Leading dimension of two-dimensional array used to store matrix A
//   - x: < type > vector with n elements if trans == [OpN] and m elements otherwise
//   - incx: Stride between consecutive elements of x
//   - beta: < type > scalar used for multiplication. If beta == 0 then y does not have to be a valid input
//   - y: < type > vector with m elements if trans == [OpN] and n elements otherwise
//   - incy: Stride between consecutive elements of y
//
// Returns:
//   - StatusExecutionFailed: The function failed to launch on the GPU
//   - StatusInvalidValue: - If m < 0, n < 0, kl < 0 or ku < 0, or - if lda < (kl + ku + 1), or - if incx == 0 or incy == 0, or - if trans is not one of [OpN], [OpT], [OpC], or - if alpha or beta are NULL
//   - StatusNotInitialized: The library was not initialized
//   - StatusSuccess: The operation completed successfully
//
// Wraps cublasDgbmv_v2.
func (h Handle) Dgbmv(trans Operation, m int32, n int32, kl int32, ku int32, alpha *float64, A *float64, lda int32, x *float64, incx int32, beta *float64, y *float64, incy int32) error {
	return dgbmv(h, trans, m, n, kl, ku, alpha, A, lda, x, incx, beta, y, incy)
}

// Dgbmv64 This function performs the banded matrix-vector multiplication y=α op(A)x+βy where A is a banded matrix with kl subdiagonals and ku superdiagonals, x and y are vectors, and α and β are scalars.
//
// Also, for matrix A op(A)={A if trans == CUBLAS_OP_NAT if trans == CUBLAS_OP_TAH if trans == [OpC] The banded matrix A is stored column by column, with the main diagonal stored in row ku+1 (starting in first position), the first superdiagonal stored in row ku (starting in second position), the first subdiagonal stored in row ku+2 (starting in first position), etc. So that in general, the element A(i,j) is stored in the memory location A(ku+1+i-j,j) for j=1,…,n and i∈[max(1,j−ku),min(m,j+kl)] . Also, the elements in the array A that do not conceptually correspond to the elements in the banded matrix (the top left ku×ku and bottom right kl×kl triangles) are not referenced. The possible error values returned by this function and their meanings are listed below. For references please refer to NETLIB documentation: sgbmv(), dgbmv(), cgbmv(), zgbmv()
//
//   - trans: Operation op( A) that is non- or (conj.) transpose
//   - m: Number of rows of matrix A
//   - n: Number of columns of matrix A
//   - kl: Number of subdiagonals of matrix A
//   - ku: Number of superdiagonals of matrix A
